--- body ---
## Context

Child of tickets/1004, blocked by 1005 (records must exist to backfill).
The `defect-confirmed` event line (spec §4.5, §5.3) is a post-merge miss
label; adopted findings are the pre-merge labels. Post-merge events are
the only source of the pair table's "neither" cell; without them,
correlation estimates skew optimistic and the labeled corpus stays
one-sided (the audition board's structural zero).

## Actions

1. Extend roar step 3's sweep ("review the fix just completed — grep for
   the same anti-pattern") with one hook: when the fix touches lines a
   past PR's review covered, append a `defect-confirmed` event line to
   that PR's attribution record: repo-relative path:line anchor, source,
   referencing PR. Zero-or-more event lines per record; no initial
   placeholder, no mutation of existing lines (append-only).
2. Backfill is factual; roar never re-judges the original review.
3. Tests: a simulated post-merge fix appends the event line to the
   referenced record and nothing else; a fix with no covered past review
   is a no-op; repeated fixes append repeated events.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A post-merge fix backfills the original PR's attribution record
- [ ] Backfilled records parse through the 1005 fixed-line reader and are
      defect-bearing per spec §4.5 (the defect-confirmed event line alone
      suffices) — visible to the 1007 read surfaces once they land

## Raid annotation (2026-10-03, imagine pass)
PREMISE: Holds, with three caveats. (1) The quoted step-3 anchor exists verbatim (`skills/roar/SKILL.md:136`); step 3 runs before step 6, so the join naturally targets only past records. (2) 1005 is not in the tree yet — no `kind: review-attribution` record exists beyond the spec example and the two `docs/memory-v8/evaluation-results.md:253,276` samples; tests must fixture 1005-format records. (3) "A past PR's review covered these lines" is computable iff reduced to "a past record has a finding anchor at (path, line)" — no "reviewer examined file P" field exists in the format; a fix on a seen-but-unanchored line is a no-op (faithful to §5.3, narrower than the phrasing). Drift hazard: anchors are line numbers as of the reviewed PR's merge — if the path changed between record-merge and fix-base, the equality join is unsound; the hook must detect it and report the anchor unresolvable (fail-loud), never fuzzy-match. Context overclaim (non-blocking): post-merge events are not the "only source of the pair table's neither cell" (a third seat's adopted finding also fills it; in the common two-reviewer game the hook fills no neither at all) — the action is unchanged.
REIMAGINE: Named sub-hook in step 3 after the anti-pattern sweep, prose in SKILL.md invoking one helper in `skills/roar/` (same shape as `enumerate-merges.py`; the append lands in the same single wrap-up bundle as step 6). Detection: the just-merged fix PR's changed (path, old-range, new-range) from `git diff --unified=0 p1...p2` (parents from step 2's existing enumeration); glob `memory/journal/**/…-review-attribution-pr*.md`, parse fixed lines only; intersect (path, anchor-line) with hunk ranges; drift guard per premise. Event line: `defect-confirmed: <p>:<l> · source: post-merge-fix · pr: <fixPR>` where `<p>:<l>` is the record's anchor verbatim (string equality at read time, §6) and `<fixPR>` is the referencing fix PR. One line per matching anchor; multiple past PRs → one line per record; repeated fixes → repeated lines (rule 5: append-only, repeatable); no match → record untouched, no placeholder; byte-append after the last line, existing lines never rewritten. Degraded paths all visible: no covered past review → no-op said in the step-3 summary; malformed line → WARN stderr + skip; drifted anchor / unresolvable record-PR → reported unresolvable.
ANTIPATTERNS: No re-judging the original review (mechanical join + append; no flipped adopted flags, no "why the reviewer missed it" prose). No rewriting records (append-only; no reformat/re-indent/"cleanup"). No deduping repeated fixes (a re-fixed line is data). No coupling to 1007 (a minimal fixed-line anchor parse inside 1006's own helper is fine; importing unbuilt 1007 tooling is the trap). No silent drops (WARN/report, never skip without a trace). No fuzzy anchor recovery (no `git log -L` archaeology — report unresolvable). No writing outside the project repo (§4.7). No firing on non-PR merges (no pr: number → skip visibly, octopus precedent).
EXIT-CRITERIA: Box 1 — the ticket's three tests against a fixture repo: (a) anchored record + simulated fix → exactly one appended line, git diff shows only that line (proves append-only); (b) fix touching no anchored line → zero file changes; (c) second fix at same anchor → second event line. Box 2 — reworded (log 2026-10-03): reduces to the §4.5 defect-bearing predicate over the shared fixed-line reader, satisfiable now and still true when 1007 lands. Verdict: keep-as-is (box 2 reword applied).

## Raid Imagine refresh (2026-10-05, next attribution batch)
PREMISE: PASS. Shared fixed-line reader now exists; use it. Explicit reviewed-revision evidence from actual durable trail establishes anchor coordinates; absent/ambiguous evidence, drift, malformed/encrypted source is unresolved, never inferred from opaque context. Whole-path equality to fix base and old-side hunk overlap provide a conservative supported join. Only commissioned defect fixes; existing repeated-event/source-preservation intent remains.
BLIND-SPOT: Multiple dated files can represent one PR. Treat duplicate PR attribution records as ambiguous, WARN and leave all untouched rather than choose/merge/multiply events. This prevents evidence corruption without inventing snapshot consolidation.
DRIFT GUARD: Existing two criteria unchanged; reviewed-SHA input is implementation evidence, not a new fixed-line field or deliverable. Correct obsolete separate-parser suggestion by reusing1005.

## Raid Plan refresh (2026-10-05, next attribution batch)

Decision: implement the narrow mechanical hook with explicitly supplied PR=reviewed-SHA coordinate evidence, not inferred opaque context or a new schema. Supplied reviewed SHA must come from the durable review trail. Verify full object identity and project ancestry, and whole file blob equality reviewed-SHA → fix first-parent/base before joining record anchors to fix old-side hunk ranges. Absence/drift warns unresolved and leaves record unchanged. Equal blobs prove coordinates under this supplied revision, not that every review necessarily used that revision; caller owns trail provenance. A branch-wide PR may contain multiple review revisions: never apply one revision to all attempts unless the trail establishes it; expose ambiguous record coordinate provenance unresolved.

Actions:
1. First meaningful test: disposable git project with recorded finding at a reviewed revision, unchanged reviewed file through fix base, and an explicit post-merge defect fix changing that old line. Invoke actual CLI with project, fix PR/commit and PR=reviewed-SHA. Assert original record bytes remain exact prefix, exactly one canonical event appended, no other project files changed, and shared reader reports label.
2. Add scripts/attribution_backfill.py importing parse_record. Explicit inputs: project repo, commissioned defect-fix PR and merge/commit identity, reviewed PR-to-SHA evidence. Derive first-parent old ranges mechanically via git, reject unresolved/ambiguous merge shape and unsafe paths, require supplied SHA resolves to full commit belonging to project history. Whole blob equality, no fuzzy tracking or rename recovery. Restrict joins to literal finding anchors; no unanchored file-coverage inference. Pure insertion old count zero means no covered old line; deletion/modification uses actual old interval.
3. Collect/validate planned append per record before write. Use original anchor verbatim, one event per distinct covered anchor/record/invocation (not reviewer), repeated invocations/fixes retained. Parse candidate text through shared reader; original bytes never rewritten. Malformed record/unknown reviewed SHA/drift/age private record warns with counts and no plaintext fallback. Successful records can proceed while unresolved records remain reported; write failure nonzero and visible.
4. Integrate Roar step3 helper root resolved in same invocation, project wrap-up tree and explicit durable reviewed revision inputs. Only identified defect fixes, not every routine merge. Capture factual unresolved/no-match/append summary in final report, bundle appends in existing single wrap-up tree.
5. Document conservative coordinate evidence and correction of §5.3 overclaim minimally in approved spec implementation note; do not change ground-truth policy or invent revision fixed-line schema.

Dependencies: merged1005 shared reader; no dependency1007/1009. Source paths: scripts/attribution_backfill.py, tests/test_attribution_backfill.py, skills/roar/SKILL.md, docs/2026-10-02-reviewer-attribution-design.md, own ticket1006 (and archive on merge): 5 content paths, 6 rename entries. No catalog frontmatter changes expected; check drift. Annotation prelude already on base avoids sibling ticket paths.

Further behavior tests: no-overlap zero changes; second fix/invocation yields second event; event-only label; two reviewers identical anchor yields one append; original file changed elsewhere before fix base fails conservative guard; shifted line/rename/deletion/unknown SHA/unresolved PR no write + visible WARN; malformed record no partial salvage; distinct old/new coordinates; multiple records only supported one mutates; opaque context remains ignored; .age unresolved without decryption. Actual git + CLI fixture provides independent positive/negative controls, not wording tests. No test merely duplicates generated line constructor; compare byte-prefix and known hand-authored expected event plus parser labels.

Acceptance: both ticket criteria proven on synthetic mechanical fixture, with limitations disclosed. Live backfill only when actual defect-fix evidence exists; no fabricated event to generate a neither cell. Full prescribed checks once after focused regressions.

Plan supplement: duplicate PR records are ambiguous; WARN and exclude all duplicates from query, backfill and coverage until separately resolved. No implicit snapshot consolidation.
